  "reasoning": "The patient presents with burning stomach pain, gas, bloating, weight loss, and loss of appetite, which are classic gastrointestinal complaints. The critical findings are dark and tarry stools (melena) and blood in the vomit (hematemesis), indicating active upper gastrointestinal bleeding. When combined with chronic burning epigastric pain, these signs strongly point to an ulcerative lesion in the stomach or duodenum. While conditions like gastroesophageal reflux disease cause heartburn, they do not typically present with significant GI bleeding. Typhoid and jaundice present with fever/diarrhea and yellowing of the skin/eyes, respectively, which are absent here. Therefore, the clinical presentation is diagnostic of peptic ulcer disease complicated by bleeding.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]peptic ulcer disease[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
